revision of #3383 with better API 


Given some actor class, `Class`, provide a typed interface to canister management as an additional curried function, `Class` hidden in a user-inaccessible `system` module:


``` motoko
actor class Class (a1:T1, ... an:TN)  {
   ....
}
```
is imported as:

``` motoko
module Lib  {
   type Class = ...;
   Class : (T1, ... TN) -> async Class;
   system : module {
      Class : 
        { #new CanisterSettings; 
          #install : Principal;
          #reinstall : actor {} ;
          #upgrade : actor {} }  ->
            (T1, ... TN) -> 
                async Class
  }
}
```
Abusing keyword system as in inaccessible field name and
where `CanisterSettings` is taken directly from the Interface Spec:

``` motoko
type CanisterSettings = {
     settings : ?{
        controllers : ?[Principal];
        compute_allocation : ?Nat;
        memory_allocation : ?Nat;
        freezing_threshold : ?Nat;
     }
}
```

The variant argument reflects the fact that you can only install code to a fresh principal, and upgrade or reinstall a canister with already installed code (i.e. an `actor {}`). The #new variant is like the default constructor (calling `create_canister`), but lets you specify the canister settings.

Since the module name `system` is an inaccessible keyword, also add sugar

`( system <exp_post> DOT <id>)` 

 desugaring to

`<exp_post>.system.<id>`

to enable explicit access to the secondary constructor.

Because of how `globalFlags` is typed, if you **do not** define any global flags then the `flags` returned from `Command.parse` will include `{ [flag: string]: unknown }`, which prevents typescript from erroring when you're attempting to access a non-existent flag.

This PR changes the default type for `flags` and `globalFlags` from `{ [flag: string]: unknown }` to `object`. Doing so removes the index signature and allows typescript to fail when accessing a non-existent flag. Unfortunately, this requires using the `object` type which is [discouraged](https://github.com/typescript-eslint/typescript-eslint/blob/v4.31.2/packages/eslint-plugin/docs/rules/ban-types.md).

The alternative to this wrapping the returned flags in a new generic type that removes the index signature,

```typescript
type Knowable<T> = {
   [ K in keyof T as string extends K ? never : number extends K ? never : K ] : T[K]
};
```

This also solves the problem but it slightly obscures what the actual flags are, e.g.

